WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.869
The train passed rapidly across the state of Iowa.

2
00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:18.321
During the night it crossed the Mississippi River and entered Illinois.

3
00:00:18.322 --> 00:00:22.859
At four o'clock the next day, the travelers reached Chicago.

4
00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:29.157
Nine hundred miles separated Chicago from New York, but trains were plentiful along this route.

5
00:00:29.158 --> 00:00:38.175
Mr. Fogg led his companions from one train to another without pause as they traversed Indiana, Ohio, Pennsylvania, and New Jersey in a flash.

6
00:00:38.176 --> 00:00:41.464
Their last train followed the Hudson River into New York.

7
00:00:41.465 --> 00:00:49.056
Late on the evening of December 11, it stopped at the station, right beside the pier where the ocean-going steamers docked.

8
00:00:49.057 --> 00:00:54.135
But the steamer for Liverpool had left port three-quarters of an hour before!

9
00:00:54.136 --> 00:00:56.005
Passepartout was crushed.

10
00:00:56.006 --> 00:01:00.496
Mr. Fogg, however, merely said, "We will decide what is best tomorrow."

11
00:01:00.497 --> 00:01:06.859
They crossed the Hudson River on a ferryboat and took a carriage to a hotel on Broadway.

12
00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:14.554
The next morning, after instructing Passepartout and Aouda to be ready at a moment's notice, Mr. Fogg left the hotel.

13
00:01:14.555 --> 00:01:17.148
It was 7:00 a.m. on December 12.

14
00:01:17.149 --> 00:01:26.623
Until 8:45 p.m. on December 21—Mr. Fogg's last minute for completing his journey—there remained 9 days, 13 hours, and 45 minutes.

15
00:01:26.624 --> 00:01:31.868
Mr. Fogg went to the banks of the Hudson to scout among the anchored ships.

16
00:01:31.869 --> 00:01:35.335
They were mostly sailing boats, which would be too slow.

17
00:01:35.336 --> 00:01:41.624
He was ready to give up hope when he spotted a trading vessel, its stack puffing a cloud of smoke, ready to depart.

18
00:01:41.625 --> 00:01:45.851
He called out for the captain, who presented himself immediately.

19
00:01:45.852 --> 00:01:53.989
He was a man around fifty, a sort of sea-wolf with big eyes and a growling voice. His name was Andrew Speedy.

20
00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:57.218
"Is your ship a fast one?" asked Mr. Fogg.

21
00:01:57.219 --> 00:02:02.530
"Yes. Its name is the Henrietta, and it is well known," said the captain.

22
00:02:02.531 --> 00:02:07.707
"Will you carry me and three other passengers to Liverpool?" asked Mr. Fogg.

23
00:02:07.708 --> 00:02:12.480
"No. I am setting out for Bordeaux, France," replied the captain.

24
00:02:12.481 --> 00:02:16.717
"I am willing to pay any sum of money," said Mr. Fogg.

25
00:02:16.718 --> 00:02:22.633
"It makes no difference," said Captain Speedy. "My destination is Bordeaux."

26
00:02:22.634 --> 00:02:25.742
"I will buy your ship," insisted Mr. Fogg.

27
00:02:25.743 --> 00:02:28.727
"No," refused the captain flatly.

28
00:02:28.728 --> 00:02:33.485
Phileas Fogg did not betray his disappointment, but the situation was serious.

29
00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:38.666
Up to this point, money had removed all difficulties. He thought hard.

30
00:02:38.667 --> 00:02:41.518
He must cross the Atlantic one way or another.

31
00:02:41.519 --> 00:02:45.249
"Well, will you carry me to Bordeaux?" he asked.

32
00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.665
"No," answered Captain Speedy.

33
00:02:47.666 --> 00:02:51.396
"I offer you two thousand dollars," said Fogg.

34
00:02:51.397 --> 00:02:55.109
"Per passenger?" asked the captain, becoming interested.

35
00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:59.860
"Yes. Two thousand dollars per passenger," agreed Mr. Fogg.

36
00:02:59.861 --> 00:03:05.623
Captain Speedy scratched his head. He had eight thousand dollars to gain without changing his route!

37
00:03:05.624 --> 00:03:09.087
"I start in an hour," he said simply.

38
00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:12.725
"We will be onboard," said Mr. Fogg, and true to his word,

39
00:03:12.726 --> 00:03:20.610
he, Aouda, Passepartout, and Mr. Fix were standing on the deck when the Henrietta lifted its anchor.

40
00:03:20.611 --> 00:03:26.452
The next day as the ship steamed eastward, a man mounted the bridge to check the ship's position.

41
00:03:26.453 --> 00:03:30.681
He unrolled the navigation charts and gazed out over the sea.

42
00:03:30.682 --> 00:03:35.427
Who was in command of the Henrietta? It was none other than Phileas Fogg!

43
00:03:35.428 --> 00:03:40.005
What had happened was simple. Phileas Fogg wanted to go to Liverpool.

44
00:03:40.006 --> 00:03:47.324
Captain Speedy would not take him. So Mr. Fogg had bribed all the sailors, and they had switched their loyalty to him.

45
00:03:47.325 --> 00:03:51.467
Captain Speedy was locked in his cabin screaming loudly.

46
00:03:51.468 --> 00:03:55.280
Passepartout thought Mr. Fogg's maneuver was simply glorious.

47
00:03:55.281 --> 00:04:00.732
It was clear, as he watched Mr. Fogg manage the craft, that Mr. Fogg had been a sailor.

48
00:04:00.733 --> 00:04:05.334
Therefore if the sea did not become too stormy, if the wind did not turn against them,

49
00:04:05.335 --> 00:04:13.940
if no accident happened to the boat or its machinery, the Henrietta would arrive in Liverpool in nine days, on December 21.

50
00:04:13.941 --> 00:04:16.717
The first days of the crossing went smoothly.

51
00:04:16.718 --> 00:04:20.870
They passed the banks of Newfoundland without encountering fog or heavy seas,

52
00:04:20.871 --> 00:04:25.823
but on the third day, the clouds darkened and the wind blew from the southeast.

53
00:04:25.824 --> 00:04:32.107
To maintain his speed, Mr. Fogg instructed the crew to increase the fires in the engine's boilers.

54
00:04:32.108 --> 00:04:38.102
On December 16 the engineer, looking serious, came on deck and spoke with Mr. Fogg.

55
00:04:38.103 --> 00:04:41.666
"You are certain of what you tell me?" asked Mr. Fogg.

56
00:04:41.667 --> 00:04:44.212
"Absolutely," said the engineer.

57
00:04:44.213 --> 00:04:47.594
The engine's fires had been burning hot for too long.

58
00:04:47.595 --> 00:04:51.635
They were running out of coal. Mr. Fogg did not hesitate.

59
00:04:51.636 --> 00:04:55.609
"Feed the fires until the coal is exhausted," he said.

60
00:04:55.610 --> 00:05:02.735
The vessel continued at full speed, but three days away from Liverpool, the engineer announced that the coal was gone.

61
00:05:02.736 --> 00:05:09.623
Mr. Fogg asked Passepartout to bring Captain Speedy on deck. The captain appeared in a rage.

62
00:05:09.624 --> 00:05:12.642
"I have sent for you, sir—" began Mr. Fogg.

63
00:05:12.643 --> 00:05:15.372
"Pirate!" yelled Captain Speedy.

64
00:05:15.373 --> 00:05:19.207
Fogg continued, "—to ask you to sell me your ship."

65
00:05:19.208 --> 00:05:22.302
"By all the devils, no!" yelled the captain.

66
00:05:22.303 --> 00:05:29.680
"But I shall be obliged to burn her," said Mr. Fogg.

67
00:05:29.681 --> 00:05:34.836
Mr. Fogg managed to find us a boat to Liverpool, though I didn't expect him to become the captain!

68
00:05:34.837 --> 00:05:40.672
Luckily, there were many boats traveling along the Hudson, one of the busiest rivers in the United States.

69
00:05:40.673 --> 00:05:45.469
The Hudson runs through New York State in the eastern part of the United States.

70
00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:51.313
It is 315 miles long and forms part of the boundary between the states of New York and New Jersey.

71
00:05:51.314 --> 00:05:59.995
The river is named after Henry Hudson, an Englishman who was sailing for the Dutch East India Company and explored it around 1609.

72
00:05:59.996 --> 00:06:05.761
Parts of the river are so beautiful that many artists have been inspired to paint it and the surrounding landscape.

73
00:06:05.762 --> 00:06:09.814
This art movement even has a name: the Hudson River School.

74
00:06:09.815 --> 00:06:13.452
As we rushed past, I would have liked to stop to paint the river too.

75
00:06:13.453 --> 00:06:22.594
But as you know, Mr. Fogg, was in quite a hurry to reach another body of water—the Atlantic Ocean!
